From 32bebdaec3b65162900f4e9a98c1beed2d2b0204 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: duck-brain <duck-brain@localhost>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 07:52:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] duck-brain P25: the duck without a PC - stops of its own, the
 DualSense's axes

For a duck run by systemd with a DualSense and nothing else:
- robotd --relax-at-start: torque off on all 15, read back, as the daemon
  starts; a boot or a restart comes up limp.
- robotd --pad-lost-relax S: relax when no client has sent a twist for S
  seconds with torque on. padd sends one at least every 0.1 s while it has
  a live pad, nothing once the pad is gone. The relax also clears the
  tick's enable, so the bring-up cannot switch torque straight back on.
- robotd --leg-load / --lift-relax-load T: the tick reads the present
  current (56..70); the legs' load is the mean |current| over hip pitch,
  knee and ankle (health feetech.leg_load, servos[].current, cur_raw in the
  journal). With T, a load under T (smoothed 0.2 s) for 0.3 s while the
  walking network drives an upright duck relaxes it. No default: T is a
  measurement.
- pad-map: Home (PS) is the stop, robot.relax on the press, in place of
  the flashlight the duck has none of.
- padd --silent-after-ms (250): a pad whose motion sensors have said
  nothing for that long is gone, though gilrs still lists it - the radio
  link lost with the sticks frozen. The IMU is kept open for it; gaps of
  100 ms and more are logged.
- pad-imu: the DualSense's raw axes to the body frame, [-Z, -X, +Y], from
  Alex's measurement of 08.11.2026 - the head follows the pad's tilt the
  right way.
